// Type-checks the test-support layer: the dev harness, the in-memory DOM and
// other doubles, the fixtures, and the tier files that stay inside the shim.
//
// Most tier files are deliberately not here. They hand shim nodes (`DomElement`)
// to production functions that expect real `Element`s, and the two are
// unassignable by construction -- a real `Element` has ~150 more members -- so
// covering them means a cast at every boundary (109 errors measured on
// 2026-10-10). They are exercised at runtime instead, where a stale field
// reference mostly fails an assertion loudly. This layer is the one where a
// stale field is silent: a fixture or harness object typed `TweetData` that no
// longer matches the type drifts without any test touching it.
//
// The three tier files below are the exception that proves the rule: they never
// cross the shim boundary (no `DomElement` reaches a production signature), so
// they type-check clean and are listed. That is what catches a dead import in
// them -- knip reads an import as a use, and nothing else parsed these files.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "include": [
    "src/env.d.ts",
    "dev/**/*.ts",
    "tests/env/**/*.ts",
    "tests/fixtures/**/*.ts",
    "tests/tiers/tier_graphql.test.ts",
    "tests/tiers/tier_i18n.test.ts",
    "tests/tiers/tier_hygiene.test.ts"
  ]
}
